#include (../Settings/defs.ves)

We already studied continuous functions on calculus class or basic analysis class.
We can also define continuity of functions on general topological spaces.

#label(defin:continuity)
useenv defin [Continuity] {
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces. We say that a function $f:X->Y$
    is \it{continuous}\index{continuous} if for every open set $V$ in $Y$,
    the set $\{f\in V\}$ is open in $X$.
}

There is equivalent statements for continuity, which will used in common.

#label(thm:continuity)
useenv thm {
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces, and $f:X->Y$ be function.
    Then the followings are equivalent.
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item $f$ is continuous.
        \item For every $A\subset X$, $f[\overline{A}] \subset \overline{f[A]}$.
        \item For every closed set $F$ in $Y$, $\{f\in F\}$ is closed in $X$.
        \item For every point $x_0\in X$, and an open neighborhood $V$ of $f(x_0)$,
        there exists an open neighborhood $W$ of $x_0$ such that $f(x)\in V$ whenever $x\in W$.
    }
    \proof{}
    [(i) $=>$ (ii)]\kern5pt
    First of all, assume that $f$ is continuous and fix any subset $A$ of $X$.
    Fix $x\in\overline{A}$ and an open neighborhood $V$ of $f(x)$.
    Since $f$ is continuous, $\{f\in V\}$ is open in $X$ and notice that it contains $x$.
    Thus there exists $a\in A\cap \{f\in V\}$ because of \Cref{prop:closure-nbhd}.
    Then we now have $f(a) \in f[A] \cap V$, and thus $f(x) \in \overline{f[A]}$ by using
    \Cref{prop:closure-nbhd} once again. Since we take any element $x$ in $\overline{A}$,
    $f[\overline{A}]\subset\overline{f[A]}$ is proved.

    [(ii) $=>$ (iii)]\kern5pt
    Fix any closed set $E$ in $Y$. In order to prove that $\{f\in E\}$ is closed,
    we will prove that the closure of $\{f\in E\}$ coincide with itself.

    Notice that if $y\in f[\{f\in E\}]$, there exists $x\in \{f \in E\}$
    such that $y = f(x)$. Since $x\in\{f\in V\}$, $f(x)\in E$ holds.
    Thus we have $y = f(x) \in E$, hence $f[\{f\in E\}] \subset E$.

    Since we assumed (ii), and $E$ is closed, we find
    #eq(eq:thm:continuity) {
        f?[\cl{\{f\in E\}}]? \subset \overline{f[\{f\in E\}]}
        \subset \overline{E} = E.
    }
    Now we fix $x\in\overline{\{f\in E\}}$. Then by \Cref{eq:thm:continuity},
    $f(x) \in f?[\cl{\{f\in E\}}]? \subset E$ holds, thus $x\in \{f\in E\}$.
    This gives $\cl{\{f\in E\}} \subset \{f\in E\}$, proving that $\{f\in E\}$
    is closed.

    [(iii) $=>$ (i)]\kern5pt
    Since $X -! \{f\in A\} = \{f \in Y -! A\}$, this part is clear.

    [(i) $=>$ (iv)]\kern5pt
    Fix a point $x_0\in X$, and an open neighborhood $V$ of $f(x_0)$.
    Since we assumed (i), the set $\{f\in V\}$ is open in $X$.
    Furthermore, clearly $x_0$ lies in that set.
    One can easily check that if we let $W = \{f\in V\}$,
    then $x\in W$ implies $f(x) \in V$.

    [(iv) $=>$ (i)]\kern5pt
    Fix an open set $V$ of $Y$. Without losing generality, we assume that
    $\{f\in V\}$ is not empty.
    Fix an element $x_0\in \{f \in V\}$.
    Then $V$ is an open neighborhood of $f(x_0)$. As we assumed (iv),
    there exists an open neighborhood $W$ of $x_0$ such that
    $f(x)\in V$ whenever $x\in W$. In other words, we find $x_0 \in W \subset \{f\in V\}$.
    Therefore, $\{f\in V\}$ is open because $x_0$ is arbitrary.

    This proves the theorem. \proved
}

Because of \Cref{thm:continuity} (d), we can consider the following definition, which is
a generalization of $\epsilon$-$\delta$ argument on $\R^d$ (or on metric spaces).

#label(defin:continuity-points)
useenv defin [Continuity at Points] {
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces. We say that a function $f:X->Y$
    is \it{continuous}\index{continuous at} $x_0\in X$ if for every open neighborhood $V$
    of $f(x_0)$, there exists an open neighborhood $W$ of $x_0$ such that
    $f(x) \in V$ for every $x\in W$.

    It is said that $f$ is continuous on $E\subset X$ if $f$ is continuous at every points
    in $E$.
}

If $Y = [-oo,oo]$, then there is a weaker version for continuity, which is called semicontinuity.
useenv defin [Semicontinuity] {
    Let $X$ be a topological space.
    We say that a function $f:X->[-oo,oo]$ is \it{upper semicontinuous}\index{semicontinuous!upper}\index{continuius!USC} at $x_0\in X$ if
    for every real $y > f(x_0)$, there exists an open neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ such that
    $f(x) < y$ for every $x\in V$.

    Similarly, we say that a function $f$ is \it{lower semicontinuous}\index{semicontinuous!lower}\index{semicontinuous!LSC} at $x_0\in X$ if
    for every $y < f(x_0)$, there exists an open neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ such that
    $f(x) > y$ for every $x\in V$.

    For a subset $E$ of $X$, we say that $f$ is upper semicontinuous on $E$ if $f$ is
    upper semicontinuous for every points in $E$;
    we define similarly for lower semicontinuity.

    We simply say USC for upper semicontinuous, and LSC for lower semicontinuous, respectively.
}

Now we are going to study properties of USC and LSC functions. We will focus on LSC specific.
However, below propositions has an analogous one for USC functions because of \Cref{prop:props-usc-and-lsc}.
#label(prop:lsc-and-open)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $f$ be $[-oo,oo]$-valued function.
    Then $f$ is LSC on $X$ if and only if $\{f > y\}$ is open in $X$ for every real $y$.
    In addition, $f$ is USC on $X$ if and only if $\{f < y\}$ is open in $X$ for every
    real $y$.
    \proof
    Assume that $f$ is LSC, fix $y\in\R$, and $x\in\{f > y\}$.
    Notice that if there is no such $x$, then automatically $\{f > y\}$ is open, so
    we can assume that such $x$ exists.
    Then the definition of upper semicontinuity gives an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$
    such that $V\subset\{f > y\}$, proving that $\{f > y\}$ is open in $X$.

    To prove the converse one, notice that $f$ is clearly LSC at $x$ where $f(x) = -oo$.
    Thus it suffices to assume that $f(x) > -oo$.
    In this case, one can take a real number $y$ such that $y < f(x)$.
    By our assumption, $\{f > y\}$ is open in $X$ which contains $x$, which shows that
    $f$ is LSC at $x$.

    One can obtain the result for USC by just following the proof in above by interchanging
    appropriate order relations. The proposition is now proved. \proved
}

#label(prop:props-usc-and-lsc)
useenv prop {
    Let $f,g:X->[-oo,oo]$ be functions. Then
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item $f$ is LSC if and only if $-f$ is USC.
        \item If both $f$ and $g$ are LSC, then $f\vee g$, $f\land g$, and $f+g$ are LSC,
        where the addition is assume to be meaningful.
        \item $f$ is continuous at $x\in X$ if and only if $f$ is both USC and LSC at $x$.
        \item Let $(f_n)_n$ is a sequence of LSC functions such that $f_n -> f$ uniformly on $X$.
        Then $f$ is also LSC.
    }
    \proof
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item Notice that for every real $y$, $\{f > y\} = \{-f < -y\}$.
        Thus \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open} immediately gives the result.

        \item Fix a real $y$. Then we have
        $$
            \{f\vee g > y\} = \{f > y\} \cup \{g > y\},\quad
            \{f\land g > y\} = \{f > y\} \cap \{g > y\}.
        $$
        Together with \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open}, we proved that $f\vee g$ and $f\land g$
        are both LSC provided that $f$ and $g$ are LSC.

        To prove that $f+g$ is LSC, it is assumed that there is no pattern $oo-oo$, otherwise
        the addition is undefined.
        Observe that
        useenv align* {
            \{f + g > y\}
            &= \{g > -oo\} \cap \{f + g > y\} \\
            &= \{g > -oo\} \cap \{f > y - g\} \\
            &= \{g > -oo\} \cap \bigcup_{x\in\R} \{f > x\} \cap \{x > y-g\} \\
            &= ?(\bigcup_{n=1}^oo\{g > -n\})? \cap \bigcup_{x\in\R} \{f > x\} \cap \{g > y - x\}.
        }
        Since $f$ and $g$ are LSC, the right most set is open by \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open}.
        Therefore, $\{f + g > y\}$ is also open, and thus $f+g$ is LSC.

        \item If $f$ is both LSC and USC, by \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open},
        both $\{f > a\}$ and $\{f < b\}$ are open in $X$ for every real $a,b\in\R$,
        which implies $\{a < f < b\}$ is also open in $X$.
        Since the topology of $[-oo,oo]$ is generated by $[-oo,a)$, $(a,b)$, and $(b,oo]$,
        this proves that $f$ is continuous.
        The converse one is obvious thanks to \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open}.

        \item Fix $y\in\R$, and $x_0 \in \{f > y\}$. Remember that we can assume that
        such $x_0$ exists, otherwise, $\{f > y\}$ will be empty and thus clearly open.
        Fix $\epsilon>0$ will be specified later.
        Since $f_n->f$ uniformly on $X$ as $n->oo$, there exists a positive integer $N$
        such that $|f_n - f|<\epsilon$ on $X$ for every $n>=N$. Especially,
        we have $f_n(x_0) > f(x_0) - \epsilon$.
        As each $f_n$ is LSC, for each $n$ there exists a neighborhood $V_n$ of $x_0$
        such that $f_n(x) > f(x_0) - \epsilon$.
        Notice that for every $n>=N$, we have $f_n(x) > f_N(x) - \epsilon$ for every $x\in X$.
        Therefore, on $V = V_1 \cap ... \cap V_N$, we have
        $$
            f_n(x) > f(x_0) - 2\epsilon,\quad \forall x\in V.
        $$
        This implies that $f(x) > f_n(x) - \epsilon > f(x_0) - 3\epsilon$ for every $x\in V$.
        Now take $3\epsilon = f(x_0) - y$ and by observe that $V$ is open.
        Hence we find an open set $V$ such that $x_0 \in V\subset\{f > y\}$, which
        proves that $f$ is LSC on $X$.
    }
    The proposition is now proved. \proved
}
